Recall that in our situation, this model is not acceptable for a publication
for 3\ reasons: (i) the identification of $\varphi$\ is bad, (ii) parameters
$\rho^{D}$ and $\rho^{S}$ captures too much information and their estimated
means are too high, revealing a potential unit root problem the current set-up
cannot handle; (iii) the acceptance ratio is roughly 40\%, we should increase
the mh acceptance rate.

Given these 3 failures:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Fix the identification problem, calibrating the parameter or adjusting
its prior information (try a beta distribution for instance).

\item To fix the information capture by AR(1) terms, our model needs to be
more backward looking.\ Add consumption habits, denoted $h$, and inflation
indexation $\xi$ in the relevant equations. The following backward looking
equations are:%
\begin{align*}
{\widehat{\pi}}_{t}  &  =\frac{\xi}{1+\xi\beta}{\widehat{\pi}}_{t-1}%
+\frac{\beta}{1+\xi\beta}\mathbb{E}_{t}{\widehat{\pi}}_{t+1}+\kappa{\hat{y}%
}_{t}+{\varepsilon}_{t}^{S},\\
{\hat{y}}_{t}  &  =\frac{h}{1+h}{\hat{y}}_{t-1}+\frac{1}{1+h}\mathbb{E}%
_{t}{\hat{y}}_{t+1}-\frac{1}{\sigma\left(  1+h\right)  }\left(  {\hat{r}}%
_{t}-\mathbb{E}_{t}{\widehat{\pi}}_{t+1}\right)  +{\varepsilon}_{t}^{D}.
\end{align*}
and estimate these parameters. Is the model fit improved? Does it fix the
excessive autocorrelation?

\item Adjust the acceptance rate.

\item Evaluate the empirical relevance of an ARMA(1,1) supply shock as in
\cite{smets2007shocks}:%
\[
{\varepsilon}_{t}^{S}={\rho}^{S}{\varepsilon}_{t-1}^{S}+{\eta}_{t}^{S}-u{\eta
}_{t-1}^{S}%
\]
where ${u}$\ is the MA term.
\end{enumerate}

